# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Build confidence at a small set of purposeful test seams rather than repeating the same assertion
through every layer.

## Outcome

The service has fast mapper and service tests, HTTP-focused MVC tests, one repository test for a
custom query, and a public-API PostgreSQL Testcontainers journey that starts from an empty schema.

## Prerequisites

- Complete [Tutorial 4](java-tutorial-04-persist-data.html)
- Docker is running for repository and integration verification

## Fast track

1. Keep mapper and service tests focused on transformations, branches, and failures.
2. Use `@WebMvcTest` for JSON binding, validation, status, body, and exception translation.
3. Test only the custom case-insensitive, bounded repository query against PostgreSQL.
4. Add one isolated Testcontainers journey through create, retrieve, and list.
5. Run `test`, `integrationTest`, `check`, and `build`; inspect coverage as a clue, not a target.
6. Complete the duplicate-ISBN challenge.

## Choose the lowest seam that proves the behaviour

The
`BookMapperTest`
proves transformations without Spring. `BookServiceTest` supplies repository doubles and exercises
reuse, retrieval, bounding, and failure rules. These tests should not inspect generated mapper code,
private methods, SQL, or incidental call order.

`BookControllerTest` uses the MVC slice because it needs Spring's actual request binding and Bean
Validation. It proves required JSON, list limits, response bodies, statuses, and stable error codes
while replacing the service with a test double.

The
`BookRepositoryIntegrationTest`
exists only for the custom case-insensitive author query and its bound. Do not test inherited
`save`, `findById`, and `deleteAll` individually; the full journey already relies on them.

The
`BookApiIntegrationTest`
starts the application on a random port. A PostgreSQL Testcontainer begins empty, Flyway creates
the schema, and a real HTTP client creates, retrieves, and lists one book. Repositories clear data
before each journey so order and IDs from another test cannot affect assertions.

## Add the tests one seam at a time

Create `src/test/java/uk/gov/justice/laa/library/tutorial/mapper/BookMapperTest.java` in the service
module:

```java
package uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.mapper;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.mapstruct.factory.Mappers;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.entity.AuthorEntity;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.entity.BookEntity;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.model.CreateBookRequest;

class BookMapperTest {
  private final BookMapper mapper = Mappers.getMapper(BookMapper.class);

  @Test
  void separatesApiAndPersistenceRelationships() {
    var request = new CreateBookRequest("Kindred", "Octavia E. Butler");
    BookEntity entity = mapper.toEntity(request);
    assertThat(entity.getId()).isNull();
    assertThat(entity.getAuthor()).isNull();
    assertThat(entity.getTitle()).isEqualTo("Kindred");
  }

  @Test
  void flattensTheAuthorName() {
    var entity = new BookEntity();
    entity.setAuthor(new AuthorEntity("Octavia E. Butler"));
    assertThat(mapper.toApi(entity).getAuthorName()).isEqualTo("Octavia E. Butler");
  }
}
```

Run it immediately:

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:test --tests '*BookMapperTest'
```

Next create `src/test/java/uk/gov/justice/laa/library/tutorial/controller/BookControllerTest.java`.
This minimal slice proves creation, validation, and stable missing-book errors:

```java
package uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.controller;

import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.when;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.*;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MockMvc;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.exception.*;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.model.Book;
import uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.service.BookService;

@WebMvcTest({BookController.class, GlobalExceptionHandler.class})
class BookControllerTest {
  @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
  @MockitoBean BookService service;

  @Test
  void createsABook() throws Exception {
    when(service.create(any())).thenReturn(new Book(1L, "Kindred", "Octavia E. Butler"));
    mvc.perform(post("/api/v1/books").contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            .content("{\"title\":\"Kindred\",\"authorName\":\"Octavia E. Butler\"}"))
        .andExpect(status().isCreated()).andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1));
  }

  @Test
  void rejectsABlankTitle() throws Exception {
    mvc.perform(post("/api/v1/books").contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            .content("{\"title\":\"   \",\"authorName\":\"Octavia E. Butler\"}"))
        .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.code").value("INVALID_REQUEST"));
  }

  @Test
  void translatesAMissingBook() throws Exception {
    when(service.get(99L)).thenThrow(new BookNotFoundException(99L));
    mvc.perform(get("/api/v1/books/99")).andExpect(status().isNotFound())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.code").value("BOOK_NOT_FOUND"));
  }
}
```

Run `./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:test --tests '*BookControllerTest'` before
adding database tests.

Create `src/integrationTest/java/uk/gov/justice/laa/library/tutorial/repository/BookRepositoryIntegrationTest.java`:

```java
@Import(TestcontainersConfig.class)
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.NONE)
@Transactional
class BookRepositoryIntegrationTest {
  @Autowired BookRepository books;
  @Autowired AuthorRepository authors;

  @Test
  void findsAnAuthorsBooksCaseInsensitivelyAndWithinBound() {
    AuthorEntity author = authors.save(new AuthorEntity("Octavia E. Butler"));
    save("Kindred", author);
    save("Parable of the Sower", author);

    var result = books.findByAuthorNameIgnoreCaseOrderById(
        "OCTAVIA E. BUTLER", PageRequest.of(0, 1));
    assertThat(result).extracting(BookEntity::getTitle).containsExactly("Kindred");
  }

  private void save(String title, AuthorEntity author) {
    var book = new BookEntity();
    book.setTitle(title);
    book.setAuthor(author);
    books.save(book);
  }
}
```

Use package `uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.repository` and import AssertJ, JUnit,
`SpringBootTest`, `Import`, `PageRequest`, `Transactional`, `TestcontainersConfig`, and both entity
types. The template's `TestcontainersConfig` supplies PostgreSQL to Spring. Run this test with:

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:integrationTest \
  --tests '*BookRepositoryIntegrationTest'
```

Finally add `BookApiIntegrationTest` in the integration-test `controller` package. Start Spring on
a random port, inject `TestRestTemplate`, clear both repositories in `@BeforeEach`, then exercise
the public journey:

```java
var request = new CreateBookRequest("Kindred", "Octavia E. Butler")
    .publicationYear(1979).isbn("9780807083697");
var created = restTemplate.postForEntity("/api/v1/books", request, Book.class);
assertThat(created.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);
Long id = created.getBody().getId();
assertThat(restTemplate.getForEntity("/api/v1/books/" + id, Book.class).getBody())
    .usingRecursiveComparison().isEqualTo(created.getBody());
assertThat(restTemplate.getForEntity("/api/v1/books?limit=1", BookList.class)
    .getBody().getBooks()).containsExactly(created.getBody());
```

Use `@Import(TestcontainersConfig.class)`,
`@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)`, and
`@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` on the class. Run the full integration task after this journey.

## Beginner primer: doubles, slices, and containers

A test double substitutes a dependency to isolate one unit's decisions. A Spring test slice loads
only the framework area being tested. A Testcontainer is a disposable real service, not an in-memory
database pretending to be PostgreSQL. Each adds cost and confidence at a different boundary.

Prefer externally observable results over implementation details. An assertion about a returned
book or HTTP error survives refactoring; an assertion about a private helper or arbitrary call order
usually does not.

## Run the feedback loops

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:test --tests '*Book*'
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:integrationTest
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:check
./gradlew clean build
```

The first command is the regular fast loop. The Docker-dependent integration task is slower and
proves migration and runtime wiring. `check` adds static checks; the clean build proves reproducible
generation and compilation.

JaCoCo highlights code not exercised, but a high percentage cannot show that assertions are useful
or boundaries are correct. Investigate surprising gaps; do not add low-value tests solely to move a
number.

## Observable verification

Temporarily change the expected missing-book code in the MVC test and confirm that only the HTTP
slice fails. Change the custom repository method to case-sensitive and confirm its focused test
fails. Restore both, then run all four commands above and observe a green result.

## Ruby and Python orientation

This is orientation, not equivalence. JUnit/AssertJ and Mockito fill roles familiar from RSpec or
pytest and unittest.mock. `@WebMvcTest` resembles a focused request/controller test; Testcontainers
provides a disposable real PostgreSQL dependency rather than a framework-specific test database.

## Challenge: specify duplicate ISBN behaviour

ISBN is optional and is not unique yet. Add one integration test that creates two books with the
same non-null ISBN and records the current behaviour. Write down the desired assertion - the second
create should return `409` with `DUPLICATE_ISBN` - but do not commit a failing or disabled test.
Tutorial 6 adds the migration and implementation first, then adds the passing regression test.


